{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261003T014546Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "a47750453f6e45044c5cc5e9", "title": "Toward provably private learning from federated data", "url": "https://research.google/blog/toward-provably-private-learning-from-federated-data/", "source": "The latest research from Google", "published": "2026-10-02T14:57:41+00:00", "is_ai": 0.98, "worthiness": 0.84, "worthiness_confidence": 0.69, "final_score": 0.84, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 8368, "delivered": true, "summary": "Google announced a next-generation Federated Learning system using Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) to provide externally verifiable differential privacy guarantees while shifting computation to servers. The system enables third-party auditors to verify privacy-preserving logic through remote attestation and public transparency logs, eliminating the need to trust server operators. For builders, this matters because it combines stronger privacy guarantees with practical improvements: Gboard reduced training time from 1-2 months to weeks, improved accuracy, and enabled larger models by offloading gradient computation to servers. The key takeaway is that TEEs with reproducible builds and published access policies on Rekor create verifiable end-to-end privacy without sacrificing training efficiency or model quality.", "jev_ms": 371.9, "jev_input_tokens": 2282, "jev_cost": 9.5844e-05, "llm_ms": 3313.0, "llm_cost": 0.002771, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261003T014546Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "0300916b911fbe76cc9315db", "title": "Inside-Out AI: Rebuilding Airbnb Behind the Scenes and Across the Guest Experience", "url": "https://www.latent.space/p/airbnb", "source": "Latent.Space", "published": "2026-10-02T14:04:49+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.7225, "worthiness_confidence": 0.89, "final_score": 0.7225, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "feed_full_text", "text_chars": 9780, "delivered": true, "summary": "Airbnb's new CTO Ahmad Al-Dahle, formerly Meta's generative AI head, is transforming the company into an \"AI-native\" organization using an inside-out approach: deploying AI internally first, then externally. Key results include 60% AI-authored code, 80% more shipped features year-over-year, and AI resolving roughly half of support tickets. Airbnb uses a multi-model strategy with at least 10 customized models, selecting different cost-performance-latency tradeoffs per use case. The company is shifting toward asynchronous event-driven agents for on-call automation and marketplace monitoring, while prioritizing that engineers understand and explain AI-generated work to maintain engineering craft.", "jev_ms": 548.9, "jev_input_tokens": 2703, "jev_cost": 0.000113526, "llm_ms": 3073.5, "llm_cost": 0.003223, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261003T014546Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "901948bef614fb42cdf74c7b", "title": "[AINews] Pi 1.0, Pi Durable, and AIE NYC", "url": "https://www.latent.space/p/ainews-pi-10-pi-durable-and-aie-nyc", "source": "Latent.Space", "published": "2026-10-02T06:40:53+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.7275, "worthiness_confidence": 0.92, "final_score": 0.7275, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "feed_full_text", "text_chars": 5651, "delivered": true, "summary": "Pi 1.0 and Pi Durable launched with significant agentic AI improvements. Pi 1.0 adds native MCP support, mid-conversation system messages, and extension support. Pi Durable ports Pi to TypeScript with crash survival via checkpointing, portability across JavaScript runtimes, concurrent branching conversations, and hot-swappable tools. For builders, this means production-ready agent frameworks with state persistence, multiplayer capabilities, and dynamic code updates without restarts. Key takeaway: Pi Durable's checkpoint-based crash recovery and pluggable storage backends enable reliable, scalable multi-agent systems.", "jev_ms": 372.2, "jev_input_tokens": 1905, "jev_cost": 8.001e-05, "llm_ms": 2897.1, "llm_cost": 0.002237, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261003T014546Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "e81b1dea7ce0fb42c9785562", "title": "Open-sourcing AstaBrief, the fast report-generation model in Asta", "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/allenai/astabrief", "source": "Hugging Face - Blog", "published": "2026-10-02T15:19:50+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.8925, "worthiness_confidence": 0.64, "final_score": 0.8925, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 18004, "delivered": true, "summary": "Allen AI open-sourced AstaBrief 8B, a specialized model for generating cited scientific reports from research questions and literature excerpts. Built on Qwen3-8B using supervised fine-tuning and direct preference optimization on 47K real user queries, it achieves 3.5× faster report generation (51.1 vs 178.5 seconds) compared to Claude-powered alternatives while maintaining competitive quality. Key insight: simple citation-density filtering of training data proved more effective than complex optimization methods for improving grounding and attribution in scientific synthesis.", "jev_ms": 562.9, "jev_input_tokens": 4117, "jev_cost": 0.000172914, "llm_ms": 2826.3, "llm_cost": 0.004591, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261003T014546Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "56c490db040147f1def4aa82", "title": "AutoSynthData: Generating Training Data for Enterprise Agents", "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/ServiceNow-AI/autosynthdata", "source": "Hugging Face - Blog", "published": "2026-10-02T04:01:31+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.9025, "worthiness_confidence": 0.67, "final_score": 0.9025, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 14435, "delivered": true, "summary": "ServiceNow CoreAI released AutoSynthData, a system that generates targeted training data for enterprise agents by identifying capability gaps from model failures and creating synthetic tasks to address them. The approach uses a stronger teacher model to validate solutions and employs multi-phase generation with rigorous verification—positive/negative checks and repair loops—to ensure task quality. In EnterpriseOps Gym experiments, fine-tuning Gemma-4-26B on 2,000 generated samples improved Hybrid domain performance by 7.2 percentage points (35% relative gain) and ITSM by 8.4 points. For builders, this demonstrates how to systematically close the gap between general model capabilities and domain-specific performance through curriculum-driven synthetic data generation grounded in real environment constraints.", "jev_ms": 298.2, "jev_input_tokens": 3321, "jev_cost": 0.000139482, "llm_ms": 3773.5, "llm_cost": 0.003965, "errors": []}
